1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,667
Chapter One hands you three scores —

2
00:00:01,667 --> 00:00:02,375
one, two, three —

3
00:00:02,375 --> 00:00:03,583
and asks for probabilities.

4
00:00:04,125 --> 00:00:06,083
But the reference code does something odd before it

5
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:07,000
does any of that.

6
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,042
It finds the largest score, and subtracts it from

7
00:00:09,042 --> 00:00:09,875
all of them.

8
00:00:09,875 --> 00:00:11,125
Every number in the middle changes.

9
00:00:11,417 --> 00:00:12,417
The answer does not.

10
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:15,042
This is Suketh Produtoor's Week One explainer for INFO

11
00:00:15,417 --> 00:00:16,792
seven three seven five.

12
00:00:16,833 --> 00:00:18,500
The narration is synthetic —

13
00:00:18,542 --> 00:00:19,542
Kokoro, running locally.

14
00:00:20,472 --> 00:00:21,888
Here is the whole idea in one breath.

15
00:00:22,180 --> 00:00:24,888
Subtracting the max makes softmax overflow-safe.

16
00:00:24,930 --> 00:00:26,263
Every number in the middle changes.

17
00:00:26,638 --> 00:00:27,638
The answer does not.

18
00:00:27,763 --> 00:00:30,180
That wording matters, because the word people usually reach

19
00:00:30,180 --> 00:00:30,972
for is stable —

20
00:00:30,972 --> 00:00:33,388
and stable claims more than this code can actually

21
00:00:33,388 --> 00:00:34,388
pay for.

22
00:00:34,430 --> 00:00:35,930
Watch where the difference shows up.

23
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:38,123
Start with why the subtraction is there.

24
00:00:38,415 --> 00:00:40,082
Two scores of one thousand.

25
00:00:40,082 --> 00:00:42,332
Exponentiate them the way the formula is written, and

26
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:44,248
Python does not hand back an enormous number.

27
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:45,040
It refuses —

28
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:46,832
overflow error, math range error.

29
00:00:47,290 --> 00:00:49,373
Double precision stops near ten to the three hundred

30
00:00:49,373 --> 00:00:51,540
and eighth, and the exponential crosses that at about

31
00:00:51,832 --> 00:00:53,040
seven hundred and ten.

32
00:00:53,082 --> 00:00:54,082
Now shift first:

33
00:00:54,248 --> 00:00:56,498
the same input returns one half, one half.

34
00:00:57,210 --> 00:00:58,168
So shift first.

35
00:00:58,168 --> 00:00:59,085
Take one, two, three.

36
00:00:59,085 --> 00:00:59,585
Find the peak —

37
00:00:59,585 --> 00:01:00,543
that is three.

38
00:01:00,543 --> 00:01:01,627
Subtract it from everything:

39
00:01:01,627 --> 00:01:03,377
minus two, minus one, zero.

40
00:01:03,377 --> 00:01:04,377
Now exponentiate.

41
00:01:04,585 --> 00:01:06,793
Zero point one three five, zero point three six

42
00:01:06,835 --> 00:01:07,877
eight, and exactly one —

43
00:01:07,918 --> 00:01:09,793
because e to the zero is one.

44
00:01:09,835 --> 00:01:10,377
Add them:

45
00:01:10,377 --> 00:01:11,752
one point five oh three two.

46
00:01:11,752 --> 00:01:13,418
Divide through, and there is the distribution —

47
00:01:13,418 --> 00:01:15,168
roughly nine, twenty-four, and sixty-seven percent.

48
00:01:16,820 --> 00:01:17,737
Why is that allowed?

49
00:01:17,737 --> 00:01:18,903
Write the shifted version out.

50
00:01:19,153 --> 00:01:21,028
Every numerator picks up a factor of e to

51
00:01:21,028 --> 00:01:22,153
the minus m over T.

52
00:01:22,487 --> 00:01:24,653
Every term in the denominator picks up that same

53
00:01:24,653 --> 00:01:25,403
factor.

54
00:01:25,403 --> 00:01:27,278
It is a common factor, top and bottom —

55
00:01:27,278 --> 00:01:29,112
so it cancels, and what is left is the

56
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:30,278
formula we started with.

57
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,153
The shift never reaches the ratio.

58
00:01:32,487 --> 00:01:33,820
That is not a numerical trick.

59
00:01:34,112 --> 00:01:35,112
That is algebra.

60
00:01:35,510 --> 00:01:37,343
The algebra says the two paths agree.

61
00:01:37,968 --> 00:01:40,052
That is a claim worth checking rather than trusting,

62
00:01:40,385 --> 00:01:42,552
so the evidence script runs both of them and

63
00:01:42,552 --> 00:01:44,343
prints the difference, with nothing rounded.

64
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:46,278
And here is the honest result.

65
00:01:46,570 --> 00:01:48,403
The two paths agree to within one point one

66
00:01:48,445 --> 00:01:50,445
times ten to the minus sixteen.

67
00:01:50,487 --> 00:01:51,070
Not zero —

68
00:01:51,070 --> 00:01:53,737
adjacent floats, one step apart, in two digits out

69
00:01:53,737 --> 00:01:54,737
of fifty-three.

70
00:01:54,778 --> 00:01:57,945
Identical in algebra; in floating point, merely indistinguishable.

71
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:00,945
Both match the chapter's published table to ten decimal

72
00:02:00,945 --> 00:02:01,903
places.

73
00:02:01,903 --> 00:02:03,237
Had I called them equal, I would have been

74
00:02:03,237 --> 00:02:04,987
wrong in the last place of two numbers.

75
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:07,117
Now the part the slogan hides.

76
00:02:07,408 --> 00:02:08,783
The shift protects the biggest term.

77
00:02:09,075 --> 00:02:10,283
Nothing protects the smallest.

78
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:12,617
Scores of zero and minus eight hundred:

79
00:02:12,658 --> 00:02:15,533
the function returns one point zero, and zero point

80
00:02:15,533 --> 00:02:15,908
zero.

81
00:02:15,908 --> 00:02:18,242
Exactly zero, for an outcome whose real share is

82
00:02:18,367 --> 00:02:20,492
ten to the minus three hundred and forty-eighth.

83
00:02:20,533 --> 00:02:22,158
And that cliff is not arbitrary —

84
00:02:22,158 --> 00:02:24,658
it sits at minus one thousand seventy-five times the

85
00:02:24,658 --> 00:02:25,658
natural log of two.

86
00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:28,020
So let us be precise about what was earned.

87
00:02:28,270 --> 00:02:29,478
What the subtraction guarantees:

88
00:02:29,812 --> 00:02:32,395
the largest weight becomes exactly one, so the denominator

89
00:02:32,395 --> 00:02:34,437
is never below one, and the top end cannot

90
00:02:34,437 --> 00:02:35,437
overflow.

91
00:02:35,437 --> 00:02:36,437
What it does not guarantee:

92
00:02:36,437 --> 00:02:38,353
that small probabilities survive at all.

93
00:02:38,687 --> 00:02:41,187
This explanation does not establish that the function is

94
00:02:41,187 --> 00:02:42,187
numerically stable.

95
00:02:42,353 --> 00:02:44,145
It establishes that it is overflow-safe.

96
00:02:44,187 --> 00:02:46,437
Those are two different claims, and only one of

97
00:02:46,437 --> 00:02:47,437
them was tested.

98
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:48,212
Your turn.

99
00:02:48,212 --> 00:02:49,337
Paste this into Claude.

100
00:02:49,545 --> 00:02:51,712
It asks for an input where the max-subtraction stops

101
00:02:51,712 --> 00:02:53,878
helping, for which end of the float range breaks

102
00:02:53,878 --> 00:02:55,878
and why, and then for the narrowest claim the

103
00:02:55,878 --> 00:02:56,920
fix actually supports.

104
00:02:57,587 --> 00:02:58,045
Run it —

105
00:02:58,045 --> 00:02:59,628
but write your own prediction of where the floor

106
00:02:59,628 --> 00:03:01,128
sits before you read the answer.

107
00:03:01,545 --> 00:03:04,045
Then watch whether Claude names underflow, or whether it

108
00:03:04,045 --> 00:03:06,003
just repeats the word stable back at you.

109
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:08,090
Shifted, not changed.

110
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:10,632
One subtraction rewrites every number in the middle and

111
00:03:10,632 --> 00:03:12,507
leaves the answer exactly where it was.

112
00:03:12,923 --> 00:03:13,757
Overflow-safe —

113
00:03:13,757 --> 00:03:15,048
and no further than that.

